  <!-- ── EU Registration Process ─────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>EU Registration for Long-term Study in Spain</h2>
        <p>If you're staying more than 3 months, here's what you need to do after arriving.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__steps">
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">1</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Arrive in Spain with Your Irish Passport</h3>
            <p>Travel to Spain — no prior visa application needed. You can enter freely using your Irish passport (or national ID card).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">2</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Enrol at Your School or University</h3>
            <p>Formalise your enrolment and obtain your acceptance/enrolment letter. This will be needed for your EU registration. Pay your tuition fees and keep the receipt.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">3</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Obtain Your NIE (Número de Identificación de Extranjero)</h3>
            <p>The NIE is your Spanish tax and identity number — essential for everything from opening a bank account to signing a rental contract. Apply at the local Policía Nacional (Comisaría) or Oficina de Extranjeros. You can book an appointment online via the Spanish government's appointment system (sede.administracionespublicas.gob.es).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">4</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Apply for Certificado de Registro de Ciudadano de la Unión</h3>
            <p>This is the EU citizen's registration certificate — your official proof of legal residence in Spain. Apply at the Oficina de Extranjeros in your city. You'll need your passport, NIE, enrolment letter, proof of accommodation, and evidence of sufficient funds.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="msv__step">
          <div class="msv__step-num">5</div>
          <div class="msv__step-body">
            <h3>Register with the Padrón Municipal</h3>
            <p>Register your address with the local ayuntamiento (town hall). The Padrón is Spain's municipal register and is required for accessing local services, healthcare, and official procedures. Bring your passport and lease agreement or accommodation confirmation.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--warn" style="margin-top: 2rem;">
        <div class="msv__alert-icon">⚠</div>
        <p><strong>Timing:</strong> You should complete EU registration within the first 3 months of arriving in Spain. Technically it is required before the 3-month mark, though in practice Spanish authorities do not automatically enforce this with immediate consequences. However, getting registered promptly is strongly recommended — it unlocks healthcare, banking, and other services you will need as a student.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Documents for EU Registration ───────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>What Documents Do Irish Students Need?</h2>
        <p>For the EU registration process — much simpler than a student visa application.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="padding: 1.5rem; background: var(--white); border-radius: 12px; border-left: 4px solid #0066cc; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
        <h3>For the Certificado de Registro (EU Registration)</h3>
        <ul style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
          <li><strong>Valid Irish passport</strong> or EU national ID card</li>
          <li><strong>Completed application form</strong> (EX-18 — Solicitud de tarjeta de residencia de familiar de ciudadano de la Unión)</li>
          <li><strong>Enrolment letter</strong> from your Spanish school or university</li>
          <li><strong>Proof of accommodation</strong> in Spain (rental contract or official housing letter)</li>
          <li><strong>Proof of sufficient funds</strong> — bank statements showing you can support yourself (see Financial Requirements below)</li>
          <li><strong>Health insurance</strong> — private insurance valid in Spain or, if you have registered with the Spanish health system, your health card</li>
          <li><strong>Fee payment</strong> — a small administrative fee (Tasa 012, approximately €10–12)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div style="padding: 1.5rem; background: var(--white); border-radius: 12px; border-left: 4px solid var(--red);">
        <h3>What You Do NOT Need (Unlike Non-EU Nationals)</h3>
        <ul style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">
          <li>Criminal record certificate — not required for EU registration</li>
          <li>Apostilled documents — Irish documents do not need apostilling</li>
          <li>Medical certificate — not required</li>
          <li>Consulate appointment in Ireland before travel — not required</li>
          <li>Visa fee — there is no visa fee for EU citizens</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Financial Requirements ──────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Financial Requirements for Irish Students</h2>
        <p>You still need to show sufficient funds — but the process is lighter.</p>
      </div>
      <p>Although Irish students are not required to provide the rigorous financial documentation required of student visa applicants, the EU registration process does ask for evidence of sufficient funds. Spain wants to confirm that EU citizens exercising freedom of movement are not going to become a burden on the state.</p>
      <p>As a student, the general benchmark is roughly <strong>€600 per month</strong> — the same figure applied to non-EU student visa applicants. In practice, Spanish authorities assess this more flexibly for EU citizens, but you should still bring:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Recent Irish bank statements (3 months) showing accessible savings</li>
        <li>Evidence of a scholarship, grant, or SUSI support payment if applicable</li>
        <li>A parental support letter with accompanying bank statements if parents are funding your stay</li>
        <li>Proof of paid tuition fees (reduces the funds threshold as living costs are the main concern)</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="msv__alert msv__alert--info">
        <div class="msv__alert-icon">ℹ</div>
        <p><strong>Healthcare:</strong> Ireland has a reciprocal healthcare agreement with Spain through the European Health Insurance Card (EHIC). Your Irish EHIC card provides access to state healthcare in Spain on the same basis as Spanish nationals. This can substitute for private health insurance in many cases. However, check current EHIC coverage scope as it can change.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── What Irish Students Can Study ───────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>What Can Irish Students Study in Spain?</h2>
        <p>As EU citizens, Irish students can study anything, anywhere in Spain.</p>
      </div>
      <p>EU freedom of movement places no restrictions on what you study or where. Irish students can enrol in:</p>
      <div style="display: grid; gap: 1rem;">
        <div style="padding: 1.3rem 1.5rem; background: var(--white); border-radius: 10px; border-left: 4px solid var(--red);">
          <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Spanish Language Schools</h3>
          <p style="margin: 0.5rem 0 0; font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--muted);">Intensive Spanish courses at accredited language schools across Spain. Under 90 days: completely unrestricted. Over 90 days: just register as EU resident after arrival.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding: 1.3rem 1.5rem; background: var(--white); border-radius: 10px; border-left: 4px solid var(--navy);">
          <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">University Degree Programmes</h3>
          <p style="margin: 0.5rem 0 0; font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--muted);">Enrol directly at any Spanish university for Bachelor's, Master's, or PhD programmes. Spanish state university fees for EU citizens are the same as for Spanish nationals — significantly lower than international student fees.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding: 1.3rem 1.5rem; background: var(--white); border-radius: 10px; border-left: 4px solid #e67e22;">
          <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Erasmus+ Programmes</h3>
          <p style="margin: 0.5rem 0 0; font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--muted);">Many Irish students come to Spain via Erasmus+ exchange programmes from Irish universities. Your Irish university's Erasmus+ office can coordinate documentation and often helps with accommodation arrangements.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding: 1.3rem 1.5rem; background: var(--white); border-radius: 10px; border-left: 4px solid #3399ff;">
          <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Vocational &amp; Professional Courses</h3>
          <p style="margin: 0.5rem 0 0; font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--muted);">Culinary arts, architecture, design, music, arts — the full range of Spanish vocational education is open to Irish citizens on the same terms as Spanish nationals.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Work Rights ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Work Rights for Irish Students in Spain</h2>
        <p>One of the biggest advantages of EU citizenship — no work restrictions.</p>
      </div>
      <p>As an EU citizen, you have the <strong>unconditional right to work in Spain</strong> — just like any Spanish national. There is no limit on working hours, no need for a work authorisation, and no restrictions on the type of work you can do.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>You can work full-time, part-time, or casual hours — no restriction</li>
        <li>No separate work authorisation application needed</li>
        <li>Self-employment and freelance work are permitted</li>
        <li>You can teach English, work in hospitality, retail, tech — any sector</li>
        <li>You need an NIE number to work (your employer will require it for payroll)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>This is in stark contrast to non-EU student visa holders (UK, US, Canadian, Australian), who are limited to 20–30 hours per week and must apply for separate work authorisation after arrival.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── After Your Studies ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section msv__section--cream">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>After Your Studies — Staying in Spain Long-term</h2>
        <p>EU freedom of movement means Irish citizens can stay indefinitely.</p>
      </div>
      <p>When your studies end, you do not need to "convert" to another visa or permit. As an EU citizen, you can simply continue living in Spain — whether you find work, start a business, or pursue further education.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>After 5 continuous years of legal residence</strong> in Spain, you qualify for <strong>Permanent Residency (Residencia de Larga Duración)</strong> as an EU citizen — a stronger, indefinite status</li>
        <li>You can work, freelance, or start a business at any point — no restrictions</li>
        <li>You retain your Irish citizenship throughout and can hold both Irish and Spanish nationality (subject to Irish nationality law)</li>
        <li>If you marry a Spanish national or have children born in Spain, additional residency and citizenship pathways open</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The Irish citizen's path to long-term life in Spain is genuinely straightforward compared to any non-EU nationality. Many Irish students who come for language courses end up building careers and lives in Spain.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

