      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="msv__faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>Do Irish citizens need a student visa to study in Spain?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>No. Irish citizens are EU nationals and have the right to live, study, and work in Spain under EU freedom of movement. No student visa is required — for short courses or long-term study. For stays beyond 3 months, Irish students must register as EU residents in Spain, but this is a registration process, not a visa application.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What do I need to do if I'm studying in Spain for more than 3 months?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>You need to register as an EU resident. This involves: obtaining an NIE number from the local Policía Nacional or Oficina de Extranjeros; applying for the Certificado de Registro de Ciudadano de la Unión (EU citizen registration certificate); and registering with the local Padrón at your town hall. You'll need your passport, enrolment letter, proof of accommodation, and evidence of sufficient funds.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can Irish students use their EHIC card in Spain?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. Your Irish EHIC (European Health Insurance Card) entitles you to access Spanish state healthcare on the same basis as Spanish nationals. This covers necessary medical treatment during your stay. It is generally accepted for the health insurance requirement of EU registration. However, once you become a registered resident, you should also register with the Spanish health system (Sistema Nacional de Salud) for ongoing care.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can Irish students work while studying in Spain?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes, with no restrictions. EU freedom of movement gives Irish citizens the same rights to work in Spain as Spanish nationals. There is no limit on hours, no work authorisation required, and no restrictions on the type of work. You will need your NIE number to be employed (required for payroll purposes), but that's a simple administrative step, not a restriction on your right to work.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do I need to apply for anything before leaving Ireland?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>No formal visa application is needed before leaving Ireland. You simply need your valid Irish passport. It is worth getting your EHIC card from the HSE if you don't already have one (free, available online). You should also arrange accommodation before arrival and have your enrolment letter ready. EU registration happens after you arrive in Spain — not before you leave Ireland.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

